;; What to compare, and how far to stretch each fixture per engine.
;;
;; The stretch factors are NOT cosmetic and they are NOT guesses. Each engine's
;; per-frame cost is the SLOPE between a short and a stretched decode, and a
;; slope resolves a per-frame cost only when the added work is large against
;; the invocation's own variation. Every number below was chosen by measuring
;; the relative standard deviation of the resulting slope on this host and
;; taking the value that measured best, not by reasoning about what ought to
;; work. Measured 2026-08-30, MacBookPro18,4 / Apple M1 Max, load1 ~370-450:
;;
;;   fixture        engine   stretch   slope rel-stdev   perfgate tolerance 0.10
;;   gop32x3        ffmpeg      400        0.21                 refused
;;   gop32x3        ffmpeg     2000        0.065                passes
;;   gop160x128     ffmpeg      200        0.138                refused
;;   gop160x128     ffmpeg     1000        0.066                passes
;;   gop320x240     ffmpeg      100        0.038                passes
;;   gop320x240     ffmpeg      300        0.038                passes
;;   gop32x3        utsushi      20        0.196                refused
;;   gop32x3        utsushi     100        0.176                refused  <- see below
;;   gop160x128     utsushi       5        0.086                passes, thin margin
;;   gop160x128     utsushi      10        0.016                passes
;;   gop320x240     utsushi       3        0.053                passes
;;
;; ## Why ffmpeg's stretches are in the hundreds and thousands
;;
;; ffmpeg's timed quantity is CPU time (`-benchmark`'s utime+stime, a getrusage
;; delta around the transcode loop, so process startup is outside it already).
;; What is left inside the differenced quantity is demux setup, a few
;; milliseconds, plus ffmpeg's 1 ms print resolution. The stretches above put
;; 300-500 ms of decode CPU on the long arm, which makes both of those a
;; fraction of a percent.
;;
;; The previous values (200/100/40) were sized against ffmpeg's WALL time,
;; where the fixed cost is process startup at 0.5-1.8 s with a standard
;; deviation of the same order. No stretch this bench could build would have
;; resolved a 10 ms decode out of that, and at 40 the 320x240 slope came out
;; with a NEGATIVE MEDIAN and a positive mean, which the report then divided
;; into utsushi's mean and printed as 41550x. That number was not a
;; measurement. See `resolve-slope`, which now refuses it.
;;
;; ## Why utsushi's stretches stay small
;;
;; utsushi's fixed cost is one already-warm JVM method call, and its per-frame
;; cost is tens to hundreds of milliseconds of CPU. A stretch in the hundreds
;; buys nothing it does not already resolve and costs hours.
;;
;; ## gop32x3's utsushi arm does not resolve at ANY stretch tried
;;
;; Raising it 5x (20 -> 100, 60 -> 300 frames) moved the relative standard
;; deviation from 0.196 to 0.176. That is the signature of PROPORTIONAL noise:
;; at 2.9 ms of CPU per 32x32 frame, JIT and GC variance is a fixed fraction of
;; the work, so a longer stream scales the noise with the signal. More stretch
;; cannot fix it and neither can more samples — perfgate's `:too-noisy` test is
;; on the samples' own relative spread, which does not shrink with n. That arm
;; is therefore reported as refused, with its number printed and marked. It is
;; the one place in this bench where the honest answer is that the quantity is
;; not resolvable here.
[{:file "gop32x3.mp4"    :picture "32x32"   :utsushi-stretch 20 :ffmpeg-stretch 2000}
 {:file "gop160x128.mp4" :picture "160x128" :utsushi-stretch 10 :ffmpeg-stretch 1000}
 {:file "gop320x240.mp4" :picture "320x240" :utsushi-stretch 3  :ffmpeg-stretch 300}]
